14. Nc3 {('N-B3')} Bh6 15. Rd1 Rad8 {('R-Q[illegible]')} 16. Bxh6 Rxd1+ 17.
Nxd1 Nxh6 18. Ne3 Ke6 19. Ke2 Rd8 20. Nf3 Nf5 21. Nxf5 Kxf5 22. Re1 Be4 23. Ke3
Bc6 24. Ke2 Be4 25. Ke3 Bc6 26. Ke2 b6 27. Ke3 h6 {('P-R3')} 28. b3 {('P-N3')}
h5 {('P-R4')} 29. Re2 a5 {('P-R4')} 30. h3 {('P-R3')} Ke6 31. Ne1 f5 32. f3 {
('P-B3')} h4 {('P-R5')} 33. Kf2 Bb5 34. c4 {('P-B4')} Bc6 {... the next pair
of half-moves did not appear in the score but have been interpolated in order
to make sense of the subsequent moves...} 35. Rb2 f4 {... continuation of
score manuscript:} 36. Ke2 Rd4 37. a3 Kf5 {('K Kt4')} 38. b4 cxb4 39. axb4 axb4
40. Rxb4 b5 41. cxb5 Bxf3+ 42. Kxf3 Rxb4 43. Kf2 Rxb5 {Source: scoresheet, in
possession of Eric Fisher (Facebook Chess Book Collectors Group), forwarded to
BritBase by Eduardo Bauza, 15 April 2020. Note, the scoresheet is in
descriptive notation and features a large number of ambiguities (see text
notes) as well as a missing pair of half-moves. BCM, Sept 1947, p288, has a
short (and inaccurate) summary of the game: "Berger [sic] had a slight
advantage in the end-game when Lenton offered him a draw. Thus the old story
was repeated once again—Berger lost." (But the result given correctly
immediately above and in the crosstable on p278) The number of moves played
given as 45, so perhaps more moves are missing.} 0-1

