{Penrose sacrificed a piece for 2 pawns, and had a draw by perpetual in the
following position. But he was determined to play for a win, and the game
continued} 21... Ng4 22. Qe2 Rae8 23. Qg2 Qh4+ 24. Kg1 Re4 25. Rbe1 (25. Rf4 {
would have given Black a lot of trouble.}) 25... Rfe8 26. Rxe4 Rxe4 27. Rf4
Rxf4 28. Nxf4 Qe1+ 29. Qf1 Qg3+ 30. Qg2 Qxf4 31. c3 Ne5 32. cxd4 Nf3+ {"and
wins". Source: BCM, June 1947, p180} 0-1

